  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nOvernight\n\nThe combination of light winds, clear skies, and dry dew point\nhas resulted in strong radiative cooling this evening. The \ncurrent temperatures are already within 5 degrees of the \nforecast low temperatures. With plenty of night to go, lowered \nthe overnight low temperatures by using the NBM 10th percentile.\nThis lowered the low temperatures by another 5 to 8 degrees. \nThey now range from the mid-30s to mid-40s.\n\nToday through Saturday\n\nAn anomalous strong 500 mb ridge will remain in control of the \narea's weather through Saturday. This high will provide dry \nweather and unseasonably warm temperatures. With the NBM running\non the cool side for the past 3 days and much of last week, \nused the 50 50 blend of the NBM 90th percentile and the NBM to \nraise the high temperatures by 2 to 5 degrees. This resulted in \nhigh temperatures ranging from the upper 60s to lower 70s today.\nThese high temperatures will gradually warm through the \nremainder of the week. On Saturday, high temperatures will be in\nthe lower and mid-70s. Low temperatures will be in the 40s \ntonight. The low temperatures will gradually warm through the\nremainder of the week. On Friday night, low temperatures will be\nin the lower and mid-50s.\n\nSunday into Monday Night\n\nA trough of low pressure will move across Manitoba and Ontario.\nThis will drag a cold front slowly southeast across the area.\nMoisture will pool along this front. This will result in rain\nacross much of the area. Rain totals will be up to a half-inch.\nThere is up to a 20 percent chance that rain totals will greater\nthan that. \n\nHigh temperatures will range from the upper 50s to upper 60s on\nSunday and in the 50s on Monday. Low temperatures will be in the\n40s on Sunday night and range from the mid-30s to mid-40s on\nMonday night.\n\nTuesday into Wednesday\n\nThere is plenty of spread in the models from Tuesday into\nWednesday. Due to this, there is much uncertainty on \ntemperatures and rain chances.",
